{
  "title": "A Binary Tree’s Nodes and Children",
  "summary": "An introduction to what nodes, roots, and child links mean in a binary tree.",
  "concepts": [
    "tree-structure-roles"
  ],
  "pages": [
    {
      "title": "Start with one node",
      "body": "A tree is made of **nodes** connected by links. For example, imagine `A` connected to `B` and `C`:\n\n```text\n    A\n   / \\\n  B   C\n```\n\n`A` is the **root**, the starting node. `B` and `C` are its **children**. In a binary tree, each node has at most two children, often called left and right. A node with no children is a **leaf**; here, both `B` and `C` are leaves.\n\nA child can itself have children. The same descriptions still apply at every level: each node is the root of its own smaller subtree.",
      "takeaway": "A binary tree has a root, and every node can link to at most two children."
    }
  ],
  "references": []
}

